    overhead isolated to \textbf{\textasciitilde{}5ms} (one Redis Lua round-trip); atomicity confirmed at
    exactly 5/40 requests allowed under 20 concurrent VUs.

  \item \textbf{Reactive architecture:} Selected Spring WebFlux over Spring MVC for its non-blocking
    event-loop model; MVC's thread-per-request model exhausts its thread pool under high concurrency
    while WebFlux pipelines requests without blocking. Throughput ceiling deliberately shifted to Redis
    network I/O, demonstrating intentional hardware/software bottleneck placement.

  \item \textbf{Atomic multi-dimensional enforcement:} Designed a hierarchical resolver evaluating four
    dimensions (Global, User, IP, and API Key) with short-circuit on first denial; each
    read-refill-decide-write sequence executes atomically in a \textbf{single Redis Lua round-trip},
    choosing Lua over application-level locking (latency penalty) and WATCH/MULTI/EXEC (retry storm risk)
    for O(1) enforcement with no race conditions.

  \item \textbf{Runtime resilience:} Built a Bearer-token-authenticated Admin API for runtime policy
    overrides with JSR-380 validation and a \textbf{DIP-compliant} \texttt{PolicyStore} interface;
    implemented \textbf{fail-open strategy} ensuring continued availability if Redis becomes unreachable.

  \item \textbf{Full observability:} Custom auto-provisioned Grafana dashboard tracking request rates,
    block rate, per-endpoint allow/deny, filter latency (P50/P95), Redis eval duration, and live token
    bucket levels per dimension; Redis fail-open alert rule included;
    \textbf{51 tests (unit + integration)} validated in CI via GitHub Actions.
\end{itemize}

